Well VMS 20 years on still cant do some thing even half as well :) Even the
cranky old pre V5 clusters had a good concept of Service Address and Service
fail over. You can build such thing on VMS but they haven't existed and
still don't. 
    Before VMS TOPS-20 was doing stuff with clusters i.e. shared mounted
file systems (structure) and could even do cute things like global shared
memory via mmap on them. They also used something called galaxy but that was
a distributed batch queue management also had a class scheduler that was
actually used. Now why is it that VMS never got a user interface for that.
VMS is not the first to use the word cluster or behave in ways we DECheads
would call  a cluster just the more memorable to younger foke. VMS like TOPS
and other good operating systems has had its day in the sun and although not
dead/disappearing its not ever going to return to its previous highs.

